1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:01,973
Lesson 9.3.

2
00:00:02,353 --> 00:00:05,194
Denoising thermodynamic models (DTM).

3
00:00:06,317 --> 00:00:11,435
Probabilistic computers sample
energy-based models very efficiently.

4
00:00:11,815 --> 00:00:15,759
But using a single energy model
to represent real data has a

5
00:00:15,759 --> 00:00:19,573
serious problem, and Jelinčič
and colleagues, at Extropic,

6
00:00:19,953 --> 00:00:24,174
proposed in twenty twenty-five
a solution inspired by

7
00:00:24,174 --> 00:00:28,236
diffusion: denoising
thermodynamic models, or DTMs.

8
00:00:28,616 --> 00:00:31,913
Today we see the problem, the
solution and the energy accounting.

9
00:00:33,017 --> 00:00:35,392
Real data have separated modes.

10
00:00:35,772 --> 00:00:40,423
A monolithic energy model that
represents them well needs deep valleys

11
00:00:40,423 --> 00:00:44,809
separated by high mountains, and
a local sampler, such as Gibbs or

12
00:00:44,809 --> 00:00:49,527
Langevin, crosses those mountains
with exponentially small probability.

13
00:00:49,907 --> 00:00:52,712
More expressivity means slower mixing.

14
00:00:53,092 --> 00:00:57,594
We measure it with a two-mode mixture,
with eighty percent of the weight

15
00:00:57,594 --> 00:01:01,909
on the right: starting from a broad
distribution, monolithic sampling

16
00:01:01,909 --> 00:01:05,536
stays at fifty-one percent
even with eight thousand steps.

17
00:01:06,650 --> 00:01:09,634
The solution is not to ask
everything of a single model.

18
00:01:10,014 --> 00:01:14,047
As in diffusion, noise is added
to the data in T steps, and each

19
00:01:14,047 --> 00:01:17,953
reverse step is represented by
a conditional energy model: the

20
00:01:17,953 --> 00:01:21,734
probability of the less noisy
version given the noisier one.

21
00:01:22,114 --> 00:01:26,082
Each of those conditionals is
almost unimodal and easy to sample.

22
00:01:26,462 --> 00:01:29,253
Complexity is built up along the chain.

23
00:01:29,632 --> 00:01:34,973
In our example, with thirty-two levels
and forty steps each, the fraction in the

24
00:01:34,973 --> 00:01:39,646
right mode rises to seventy-five
percent, approaching the true eighty.

25
00:01:40,750 --> 00:01:45,058
The proposed architecture, DTCA,
implements each conditional as

26
00:01:45,058 --> 00:01:49,024
a sparse Boltzmann machine on
a seventy-by-seventy grid of

27
00:01:49,024 --> 00:01:52,648
binary variables, each
connected to twelve neighbors,

28
00:01:53,028 --> 00:01:56,117
with some variables for the
data and the rest latent.

29
00:01:56,497 --> 00:02:01,607
Each node is an all-transistor random
bit generator, with a sigmoidal response

30
00:02:01,607 --> 00:02:06,717
measured in the lab and a decorrelation
time of about one hundred nanoseconds.

31
00:02:07,097 --> 00:02:09,967
The grid is bipartite, so a full Gibbs

32
00:02:09,967 --> 00:02:13,140
sweep takes about two decorrelation times.

33
00:02:14,250 --> 00:02:19,179
The energy model is E = T
K_mix L² E_cell: number of

34
00:02:19,179 --> 00:02:24,771
denoising steps, times Gibbs
sweeps, times number of cells,

35
00:02:25,151 --> 00:02:29,082
times the energy of each cell,
estimated at about two femtojoules.

36
00:02:29,462 --> 00:02:34,588
With eight steps, two hundred fifty
sweeps and seventy-by-seventy cells, that

37
00:02:34,588 --> 00:02:38,982
is about twenty nanojoules per
sample, and about 0.4 milliseconds.

38
00:02:39,362 --> 00:02:44,121
With that accounting, the authors estimate
matching the quality of GPU models

39
00:02:44,121 --> 00:02:48,385
on binarized Fashion-MNIST with
about ten thousand times less energy.

40
00:02:49,517 --> 00:02:50,801
How to read that figure?

41
00:02:51,181 --> 00:02:55,609
It combines real measurements of the
bit generator with physical models and

42
00:02:55,609 --> 00:02:59,684
simulations: it is an estimate, not
a measurement of a complete chip.

43
00:03:00,064 --> 00:03:03,374
The GPU energy is computed
by dividing operations by the

44
00:03:03,374 --> 00:03:06,567
manufacturer's specification,
which underestimates it.

45
00:03:06,947 --> 00:03:08,915
And the benchmark is small.

46
00:03:09,295 --> 00:03:14,917
For larger tasks, the authors combine a
DTM with a neural network: on CIFAR-ten

47
00:03:14,917 --> 00:03:19,685
they match a purely neural model
using a network ten times smaller.

48
00:03:20,065 --> 00:03:23,539
Promising, and still to be
demonstrated on complete hardware.

49
00:03:24,650 --> 00:03:30,762
DTMs serve for generative modeling on
probabilistic hardware; during training,

50
00:03:31,142 --> 00:03:35,880
an adaptive correlation penalty
keeps the conditionals easy to mix;

51
00:03:35,880 --> 00:03:40,053
and for larger data they are
combined with neural networks.

52
00:03:40,433 --> 00:03:45,223
In lab fourteen you will
train a small DTM in THRML.

53
00:03:46,350 --> 00:03:49,397
Common mistakes: using
a monolithic model for

54
00:03:49,397 --> 00:03:52,241
multimodal data and trusting short chains.

55
00:03:52,621 --> 00:03:57,039
Using too few steps, so the
conditionals become multimodal again.

56
00:03:57,419 --> 00:04:01,279
Confusing the hardware decorrelation
time with the number of sweeps.

57
00:04:01,659 --> 00:04:05,065
And quoting the ten thousand times
as if it were a chip measurement.

58
00:04:06,183 --> 00:04:09,229
In summary: a monolithic
energy model mixes

59
00:04:09,229 --> 00:04:12,133
worse and worse as it becomes expressive.

60
00:04:12,513 --> 00:04:17,060
A DTM chains easy conditional
models, like diffusion steps,

61
00:04:17,060 --> 00:04:21,375
and each one is sampled with
Gibbs on a transistor grid.

62
00:04:21,755 --> 00:04:25,264
Energy is estimated with
T K_mix L² E_cell, and the

63
00:04:25,264 --> 00:04:30,286
ten-thousand-fold advantage is a
promising estimate on a small benchmark.

64
00:04:31,417 --> 00:04:35,539
To practice: explain why a
local sampler mixes slowly.

65
00:04:35,919 --> 00:04:38,042
Write the energy of a conditional.

66
00:04:38,422 --> 00:04:40,640
Compute the energy for four steps.

67
00:04:41,020 --> 00:04:44,765
And list three caveats about
the ten-thousand-fold estimate.
